{"title":"Subjectwise Test-9 Part Syllabus GATE 2026 EC Digital Communication Systems","fileName":"050_Subjectwise Test-9 Part Syllabus GATE 2026 EC Digital Communication Systems.html","branch":"ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING","series":"GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series Electronics Engineering","provider":"MADE EASY","duration":5400,"questions":[{"qnum":1,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>A Discrete Memoryless System, X has four symbols <img src=\"/media/8bbdcc54362adf25e98125543160c1fb78cac407.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> with their respective probabilities <img src=\"/media/afb9233d05f3ab088da07a07ab31ca180daab72a.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /><br />\nThe difference between amount of information contained in the message <img src=\"/media/553abccc80119840d80883b077c5b13612c3d1e3.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> is 1 bits, then the value of X and Y respectively are</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 0.4 and 0</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 0.2 and 0.2</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 0.3 and 0.1</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> None of these</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/5f5023afccfde6fbac7c2f2192e5d66cfaad10cd.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":2,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>A source produces ‘n’ symbols with unequal probabilities <em>P<sub>n</sub></em>(<em>x</em>) such that the probability of a symbol is twice of that of the previous symbol and half to that of the next symbol. The entropy of the source and probability of last symbol are respectively,<br />\n(Take probability of first symbol as <img src=\"/media/b09df7676466944e3ee699470517289de93c0c80.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> )</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/3afceeba764c997b98b541af23bac5fae3d695b9.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/378e08160d2945475f7805d5ea05d66ff4fa1d84.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/6630dc2d7040ab332fed714aad143f9a49eb23ab.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/a8acf3e942808adc7f37e201f614f9af3855be57.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/6932085e076b63a8831e38cb8d4fe717dc04d0d7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/e3857475b208b3a8dd0a33b655b3cf406ea955b3.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":3,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p><em>X</em> and <em>Y</em> are two independent random variables with zero mean and unit variance. Another random variable Z is defined as Z = 4<em>X</em> + <em>Y</em>. Then mean and variance of Z are respectively,</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 0; 1</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 0; 17</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 1; 0</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 0; 5</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/548f8db0a252f8155eff8130288a8ddd6badecf8.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":4,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>A (6, 3) systematic linear block code encodes the information sequence <img src=\"/media/17a0c5948956a0d0740288ee7f923c0b91b4436f.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> into code word <img src=\"/media/678c4442f6f421df820987b785cf101a0c3b9a26.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> such that <em>C</em><sub>4</sub> is a parity check on <em>C</em><sub>3</sub> and <em>C</em><sub>1 </sub>to make the overall parity even i.e, <img src=\"/media/dc728013e99c8c7a60b26109318ce6d5c6f0dc22.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> Similarly <em>C</em><sub>5</sub> is a parity check on <em>C</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>C</em><sub>2</sub>, and <em>C</em><sub>6</sub> is a parity check on <em>C</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>C</em><sub>3</sub>. The minimum distance of this code is,</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 3</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 2</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 4</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 5</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/4b53bf84b662b47267b69a6693e99741dd2c40cd.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":5,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>If x is a continuous random variable whose probability density function is given by</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/c8bb5d0fa15922d0827841f8f5b0a717d304a21f.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div>If <em>A</em> > 0, the value of ‘<em>A</em>’ is</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 1</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 0.5</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 0.25</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 0</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/c6d19d20e619e7d1d2b129f2cc71f7636aee2831.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/8ff061567aa205e27771749de1a072304d6859e5.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":6,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>The power spectral density of a stationary random process <em>x</em>(<em>t</em>) is given by,</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/bba137943d98f30785f3cbbf43850a3545abea19.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div>If the average power of the random process <em>x</em>(<em>t</em>) is equal to 4 watts, then the value of ‘<em>A</em>’ is</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 4</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 2</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 6</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 8</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/bbce41484d37bfd45f6bb4e29e2cfe63168344d6.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":7,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider the signal space diagram shown below for a binary digital signalling scheme,</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/28a29cbe13af648aed70ddc4c170c2333dc06e95.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>If the noise power spectral density is <img src=\"/media/9c9b71bd0fd0f59c888f0a176aebc01e7738a0ec.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> and bit energy is 4μ Watt-sec, then the probability of error is <img src=\"/media/c215dff8c6d0043f366c89345fd7b8989f00216a.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 2.25 × 10<sup>–3</sup></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 7.9 × 10<sup>–6</sup></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 17.2 × 10<sup>–3</sup></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 6.2 × 10<sup>–6</sup></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/d296b145d6b36f5d2595d8e749dd5ab2ebed8a31.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":8,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider a AWGN channel with a bandwidth of 10 MHz and a channel capacity of 60 Mbps. The channel capacity if the channel bandwidth approaches to infinite will be</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 630 Mbps</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 90.72 Mbps</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 63 Mbps</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 907.2 Mpbs</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/ce70a347a687c0e44bb1bf971bee3d1912a65203.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/e60fe51f04d8139fd9cca9dd2f49b32723be0bd9.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":9,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider the sum of two ergodic noise process <em>n</em>(<em>t</em>) = <em>n</em><sub>1</sub>(<em>t</em>) + <em>n</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>t</em>). The DC value of <em>n</em><sub>1</sub>(<em>t</em>) and <em>n</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>t</em>) are –5 V and 5 V respectively. The ac power of <em>n</em><sub>1</sub>(<em>t</em>) and <em>n</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>t</em>) are 2 W and 2.7 W respectively. The cross correlation between <em>n</em><sub>1</sub>(<em>t</em>) and <em>n</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>t</em>) is 5 V for τ = 0. The power of <em>n</em>(<em>t</em>) is</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 50 W</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 27 W</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 64.7 W</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 17.7 W</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/8995b621e02b4bac740f706e83ffbb266be257f2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":10,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Consider the signal shown below:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/b2d029d2123019f81c5981a46ed9fec6d4cd9eba.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>This signal is passed through an AWGN channel with two sided noise power spectral density of  <img src=\"/media/d8c1199e5054a0c537c2ba0ece92638c410f989a.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> and received by a filter matched to s(t). The maximum signal to noise ratio possible at the output of the filter is <img src=\"/media/21588084943f033819e7c30a5ab4ef9928c8b3df.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> Then, the value of <img src=\"/media/f554470a932550839744b4caef45bcfe63b78235.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" />is ________.</div></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"1","high":"1"},"solution":"<p>1 (1 - 1)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/fe74a1a77281157f5c13a61da92085efd8da8f2e.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":11,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>The joint probability density function of random variables <em>X</em> and <em>Y</em> is given as</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/cfd10e38060063ccee15fa2b2bc19561e4f26031.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div>The probability that <em>X</em> ≤ 0, <em>Y</em> ≤ 1 is <img src=\"/media/6120bc7d552cf563c220956398c84842b8800ff4.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> Then the value of z is _________.</div></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"1","high":"1"},"solution":"<p>1 (1 - 1)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/4187fff1b04f80ed51919b93573557bf5677874b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":12,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>The signal <em>X</em>(<em>t</em>) is given as input to a matched filter as shown below:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/a873333642125a26397c203c6fde99feadbd0b5a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>If the maximum value of the filter output is 6, then the value of <img src=\"/media/5a28d3ef6383b85e645edbd7ecd5d10cbca447e3.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> is __________.</div></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"1","high":"1"},"solution":"<p>1 (1 - 1)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/8e7dd3da591d8c7ed8e7216ef29864b999f4a367.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":13,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>A communication channel with AWGN has a bandwidth of 4 kHz and an SNR of 31. Due to noise, the bandwidth efficiency reduces to half. For error free transmission, the ratio of energy per bit transmitted to noise power spectral density should be atleast equal to __________. (rounded upto two decimal places)</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"1.86","high":"1.86"},"solution":"<p>1.86 (1.70 - 1.99)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/3533aeb008128c4d9568579feddf16ea5c480037.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n</div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":14,"type":"NAT","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Consider two binary symmetric channels with crossover probability of 0.35 and 0.45 are cascaded, then the crossover probability of the resultant BSC will be _________ (rounded upto three decimal places)</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"0.485","high":"0.485"},"solution":"<p>0.485 (0.400 - 0.550)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/4273867c299b80bb09baaac50258df5795c2d314.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":15,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Given two signals <em>X</em><sub>1</sub>(<em>t</em>) = 1.5 cos 2000πt and <em>X</em><sub>2</sub>(t) = 3 cos 5000π<em>t</em> resultant signal <em>Z</em>(<em>t</em>) = <em>X</em><sub>1</sub>(<em>t</em>) + <em>X</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>t</em>) sampled with the sampling frequency of 100 kHz and then transmitted over the channel.<br />\nThen, the value of the guard band in the sampled signal spectrum is equal to _________ kHz.</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"95","high":"95"},"solution":"<p>95 (94.5 - 95.5)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/3e9eebe766fa9905ccc56de8261c555287695b9c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div> </div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":16,"type":"MSQ","correct":"B,D","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Which of the following relations w.r.t autocorrelation function is/are incorrect?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Inverse Fourier transform of power spectral density will give autocorrelation function.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/42373d36895a829ebbe231fd9c99c5b76e4f7d52.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/39816915e76dda24a345a53f0145711727a3be48.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/9688dd1977d3b2dfaae4fbd4fc2abe49d8b75cdf.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b, d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/5609db54d835fa1be473030aa15a4ca14b2d490c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":17,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>An on-off binary system uses the pulse waveforms:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/4bb1c31b5cf1d5e0c5b54f0f8c4ffcd4ef987caa.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n</div>\n<div>Assume T = 2μs. Additive white noise with a power spectral density η/2 = 10<sup>–15</sup> W/Hz is added to the signal. If <img src=\"/media/24627598799a7e8cdb912374fe9280ccba44fb20.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> the probability of error is</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/852a2fed0823d4593a175b11f1417c618500b133.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/99c87c9078a771b63d94ff429e813e06fcbc208b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/737960ddd1ddb3a3aa776afddc67af4980d93402.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/c8cf8d740cfb5fdd678d0747bd94d99412ad1b0c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/9a0d7960c2ae0b1d69a81f3a08d230da55c17028.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/95005e3d94242b64e9db04ad12b0de29744a0448.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":18,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Let <em>X</em> and <em>Y</em> be defined by<br />\n<em>X</em> = cos θ and <em>Y</em> = sin θ<br />\nwhere θ is a random variable uniformly distributed over [0, 2π].<br />\nWhich of the following options is correct?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <em>X</em> and <em>Y</em> are uncorrelated and dependent.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <em>X</em> and <em>Y</em> are uncorrelated and independent.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <em>X</em> and <em>Y</em> are correlated and dependent.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <em>X</em> and <em>Y</em> are correlated and independent.</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/54136d701ebff16a3a085267dc975a6f65616d9b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/a263b52abb1b15cbb06ac67dcb2862f4d3a1193c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":19,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Consider <em>X</em>(<em>t</em>) to be White Gaussian noise with zero mean and autocorrelation function <img src=\"/media/27041bc0d3e865215d014fde463e9cab4a176596.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" />It is passed through an LTI system whose impulse response is <em>h</em>(<em>t</em>) = sinc(2π<em>t</em>). The power of the output random process <em>Y</em>(<em>t</em>) is</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/29ab93df96d8670f4232c9f67ed81dd6c0abc612.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/623c7a9af9fcf0f519458f322601361dfa78cfbb.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/cf85fb24bf9bedc52f08b349b3f9a55fd110867f.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/d82bcac1ce086a3fdc71ea9fadac803dc6fba3b2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/797804a10ae9f003e944ec10b9ef56b75f97b0be.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/16afb6e6cab0a7aaf42e04514ad04a0618719a78.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":20,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>A linear Delta modulator is designed to transmit speed signal bandlimited to 4 kHz. Sampling rate is 10 times to Nyquist rate and step size is 100 mV. The system is tested with 1 kHz sinusoidal signal. Maximum amplitude of the test signal allowed to avoid slope over load will be ________</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 50 mV</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 318.31 mV</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 512.6 mV</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 1.27 V</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/2c3b1d8303e79e09dec3a823cf0f3a39d5cf1b89.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":21,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>For a (5, 2) linear block code, the generator matrix is given as</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/782066a99add98e2eb627b7f6b36698ae1381b63.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>Then the parity check matrix for this code is given as</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/4b558fb91ca9667af6ffb3cc301e77f2bf186613.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/8769b3bd7c170f6d7515acb6cb42a2d0de5608d9.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/c122471695e36bf4fc8db774096c305dd5f0d9d8.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/d12199372b1704c2ef55dda504d8a792934dbb01.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/eb4f480a44d517f2a2d35e8fb736814711c8b69b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":22,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>An analog signal is sampled, quantized and transmitted using a PCM system. If the tolerable error in sampled amplitude is 0.1% of the peak to peak full scale value, then the minimum number of binary digits required to encode a sample to fulfill this requirement is equal to</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 6</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 7</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 8</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 9</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/8ec3933992da3395a6c0f7586af11fe742c4ba7d.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":23,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Two continuous random variables <em>X</em> and <em>Y</em> are related as <em>Y</em> = 4<em>X</em> + 2. The correct relation between the differential entropies of the two random variable is equal to</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <em>H</em>(<em>Y</em>) = <em>H</em>(<em>X</em>)</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <em>H</em>(<em>Y</em>) = 4<em>H</em>(<em>X</em>) + 2</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <em>H</em>(<em>Y</em>) = <em>H</em>(<em>X</em>) + 2</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <em>H</em>(<em>Y</em>) = 4<em>H</em>(<em>X</em>)</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/63e1072cb6ff47cc33f3ad7afaa8d8ae09a37547.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/a98b4f2f3df9de356db36cf0eb71cb617422cbd4.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":24,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Bandwidth of an input signal to the PCM is restricted to 4 kHz. The input signal varies in amplitude from –3.8 V to +3.8 V. It has an average power of 30 mW. Required signal to noise ratio is 20 dB. The PCM modulator produces binary output. Assuming the use of a uniform quantization, the minimum number of bits required per sample is equal to</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 5 bits</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 6 bits</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 7 bits</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 8 bits</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/0a2b1614d3ac9bb69fd342e3a00802f9aebc7cba.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/aebc8b32d3aa5acfb618ce2024f9fcf8ce6ca7f7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":25,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Consider two signals shown in the figure below:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/ec4db2088384b9675f789180656f287a12423ca4.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>The signal <em>s</em><sub>1</sub>(<em>t</em>) is applied to the input of a matched filter receiver whose impulse response is matched to the signal <em>s</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>t</em>), then the output of the matched filter can be represented as</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/f008f84b1030f3485af767f848990c1955c85a08.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/3daed7a3bebea8187d7a02d2643bf666be8472d5.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/5ae2324d370d14803cf32547682d021329e15389.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/9de6f110fedeece18d3a33fe9b5e1292404d9665.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/3eaac4fd6a6199f4d62cb5fc91d30acc0f2f115b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":26,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Consider a source <em>m</em>(<em>t</em>), whose amplitude statistics is defined as</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/022f1bc31d9bc9648a517d05e9c1b6b3e0d3864b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>An optimum 3 bit quantizer is designed to sent the message signal over a baseband channel. The signal to quantization noise will be equal to</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 20.16 dB</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 16.43 dB</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 9.2 dB</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 12.51 dB</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/7221ac4b68c30926fdad17a688938ab679bd9c21.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":27,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Consider the three signals shown in the figure below:<br />\nWhich of the constellation diagram can be used to correctly represent the three signals?</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/4cd5e34a2edc7dc148e77a9477b5878538e33746.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/3a17eacf0acc88ad33116ea09a9d04eca19055ee.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/0abbf90518046be78eea84579e5cd22686aa498d.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/15f701d6f278f4552cf4c38b9cf4b5bcdc768949.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/5311427ed99a7305004e03b5f0b9820e112102c6.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/1128f530b0604c564692412abdcdcc027a669750.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":28,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>A signal <em>m</em><sub>1</sub>(<em>t</em>) is bandlimited to 5 kHz and three other signals <em>m</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>t</em>), <em>m</em><sub>3</sub>(<em>t</em>) and <em>m</em><sub>4</sub>(<em>t</em>) are bandlimited to 2 kHz each. These signals are to be transmitted by means of time-division multiplexing. If the commutator output is quantized using 512-level quantizer and the result is binary-coded, the minimum output bit rate for faithful transmission is _________ kbps.</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"198","high":"198"},"solution":"<p>198 (198 - 198)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/1df88c4ec739d8868a174791e8383864a2501392.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/ff5cdb618cbaeadd0d299d7c81e32a3ec1a20e58.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":29,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Consider a signal <em>x</em>(<em>t</em>) defined as:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/1357e39ac55cd6b7a01ec3b4c16a97413d173532.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div>The 80 percent energy containment bandwidth of the signal is _________ rad/sec.</div></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"4","high":"4"},"solution":"<p>4 (4 - 4)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/c2a23eae42a2dbc104c1660dad7996ce76888f41.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":30,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>A carrier system used in digital telephony system time multiplexes 20 voice channels. Each voice channel is sampled at 10 kHz and a ‘n’ level quantizer is used. If 20 additional bits are added at the end of the frame for the purpose of synchronization, then the resultant transmission rate is<br />\n1.8 Mbps. Number of Quantization levels ‘<em>n</em>’ is _________.</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"256","high":"256"},"solution":"<p>256 (256 - 256)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/1c0872149c8035a8d99409da6d1bbfc2ead7d026.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/ce135b7095e30e5c871aac437c1c5d4285bbc3fb.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":31,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Let <em>X</em>(<em>t</em>) and <em>Y</em>(<em>t</em>) be defined by<br />\n<em>X</em>(<em>t</em>) = A cos ωt + B sin ωt<br />\n<em>Y</em>(<em>t</em>) = B cos ωt – A sin ωt<br />\nwhere ω = 1 rad/sec and A and B are independent random variables having zero mean and<br />\nvariance of 5. The cross-correlation of X(t) and Y(t) for a lag of τ = 5 sec is ________. (Rounded upto two decimal places)</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"4.79","high":"4.79"},"solution":"<p>4.79 (4.20 - 5.50)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/1f864956e9d973b354586daf5bc1a9d2df582f97.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":32,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,B,C","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>An LTI system with impulse response <em>h</em>(<em>t</em>) produces the output <em>Y</em>(<em>t</em>) when a WSS random process <em>X</em>(<em>t</em>) is applied to the input, then which of the below given relations is/are correct?</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/3df564faecb56edefeb4ff195ab77046e1d3973c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>[Here, R denotes the correlation function and S denotes the power spectral density]</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> a</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> b</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> c</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> d</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/e220b98dd7ee1296a2b5d11334c1712e637ead25.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/8f5a027c31efbf40e70c4e6ee091d356c9a08676.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":33,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,B,C","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Consider the signals shown in the figure below:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/2fa04220ce3e45329f512cf28496e371ee83eb2a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>If these two signals are forming a set of orthonormal basis functions, then which of the following statements is/are correct?</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/7adfefb3caae1183943a3864f1b1c70ba0c6356b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/091ce15fe56578c39ead467f19059bfbd8d88f6b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/c588bd5b8ba5343c04a758c51ca9f761a3168fad.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> Gram-schmitt procedure is unique.</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/e4605d43b0d7ba09675ac1700eb80ea8ccaee28c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"}],"questionCount":33,"parsedAt":"2026-11-10T19:39:15.550Z"}